## 9. PIR Quality Checklist (Validation Criteria)

### What It Is
A structured self-assessment checklist that PIR champions use to validate PIR completeness before approval.

### How It Works

**Mandatory Quality Checks:**
- [ ] Narrative starts with ≤3 sentences: timing, impact, root cause, learnings
- [ ] Root Cause section starts with simple factual statement in<3 sentences
- [ ] No ambiguous wording, adjectives, weasel words; acronyms explained
- [ ] Timeline starts with customer impact or first alert
- [ ] Any gaps in timelines called out in root cause or learnings/action items
- [ ] TTD and TTR clearly marked in timeline with relevant tags
- [ ] No mention of customer or individual names
- [ ] Prior PIRs whose unactioned items could have prevented this incident are referenced
- [ ] All learnings have at least 1 action item
- [ ] At least 1 action item adds automated testing around root cause
- [ ] Any breached SLOs categorized as critical or non-critical
- [ ] Document stands alone without prior knowledge of internal workings
- [ ] Highlights "pattern of problems" posing similar risk
- [ ] Root cause investigation only stops with: Root Cause / Insufficient Data / Not Resolvable Organisationally
- [ ] Analysis does not end on human error/human action
- [ ] Each learning or gap has at least 1 action item

### How It Applies
The `pir_auto_drafter` should:
- Run this checklist as a validation pass before outputting the draft
- Flag incomplete sections with specific guidance on what's missing
- Generate a "PIR Quality Score" (% of checklist items satisfied)
- This becomes the gate for autonomous submission vs. human review escalation

