## Finding 12: AI Programming Ladder — HITL/HOTL/YOLO Spectrum

### What it is
A conceptual model for human involvement levels in AI-assisted programming, providing the vocabulary for the HITL→HOTL transition.

### How it works

| Level | Name | Human Involvement | Atlassian Status |
|-------|------|------------------|-----------------|
| 1 | AI Autocomplete & Chat (Mid-loop) | AI predicts next line or answers question | ✅ Available |
| 2 | Autonomous Agent (HITL) | Single agent reads files, writes code, runs commands within sandbox | ✅ Available |
| 3 | Subagents (HITL→HOTL) | Main agent delegates sub-tasks to specialists | ✅ Available |
| 4 | Agent Teams (HOTL) | Specialized agents collaborate, share context, self-correct | ⚠️ Partially Available |
| 5 | Autonomous Agents (YOLO) | Agents design architecture, set goals, deploy projects end-to-end | 🔮 Future |

**Key insight:** "The higher you climb, the riskier the fall, reflecting the increased demands on tooling and frameworks required to move up the ladder."

### How it applies

Maps to tier classification:
- Level 1-2 (HITL): Every action is effectively Tier 2-3 (human approves each step)
- Level 3 (HITL→HOTL transition): Sub-tasks may be Tier 1 within bounds; parent task remains Tier 2
- Level 4 (HOTL): Known-pattern actions are Tier 1; novel decisions Tier 2; boundary violations Tier 3
- Level 5 (YOLO): Tier 1 is the default; only policy violations and strategic ambiguity trigger Tier 2/3

**Source:** [The AI Programming Ladder](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/~ipopovic/pages/6516576192)

