## 6. Authentication & Access Control

**Source**: [Read Features (Development)](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/agd/pages/5795966627), [Alpha Release Announcement](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/MLP/blog/2025/08/11/5676238440), [ML Registry SDK Design](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/MLP/pages/4924999902)

### 6.1 Auth Pattern: ASAP (Atlassian Service Authentication Protocol)

- MLFS uses **ASAP** authentication (standard Atlassian service-to-service auth)
- Replaces Tecton's API KEY-based auth
- Access control at **Feature View** and **Feature Service** level via `consumers` list
- Only machine access (micros services listed in consumers) allowed for reading RAW UGC features
- Access managed via Slauth: `atlas slauth curl --aud=ml-feature-store -g <group> -- ...`

### 6.2 Access Groups (from comparison report reproduction steps)
- Admin access group: `micros-sv--ml-feature-store-dl-admins`
- Use case access via SSAM groups: e.g., `mlp-search-relevance-dl-ml-studio-access`

### 6.3 Feature View Level Access Control
```yaml
consumers:
  - "micros/xpsearch-aggregator"
  - "ml-studio/search_relevance/released"
```

### How It Applies to Tool Design
The wrapper tool must:
1. Use ASAP token generation (via `atlas slauth` or programmatic ASAP issuer)
2. Include the tool's service identity in the `consumers` list of any feature view it needs to read
3. The tool's ASAP issuer must be pre-approved in MLFS access configuration

---

## 7. Rate Limiting

**Source**: [Rate Limiting Feature Views in MLFS](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/MLP/pages/5329000421), [RFC: MLFS Serving Architecture](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/MLP/pages/5218224830), [RFC Rate Limiting Using Heimdal](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/~712020b57ad88626724744b8c93e5215cb7dda/pages/5923905182)

### 7.1 Current Limits
- **Total ERS rate limit**: 90,000 RPM per region (TDP ERS)
- **MLFS allocation**: 75% = 67,500 RPM per region total across all feature views
- **Rate limiting approach**: Per-feature-view level, configurable throughput allocations
- **Enforcement**: Service Proxy Ingress + Heimdall rate limit policies (fixed window algorithm)

### 7.2 Rate Limit Headers
When rate-limited (HTTP 429):
- `X-RateLimit-Limit`: Allowed requests in current period
- `X-RateLimit-Remaining`: Remaining requests in current period
- `X-RateLimit-Reset`: Next period reset time (ISO 8601)

### 7.3 ML Studio Orchestrator Rate Limits
- Endpoint-specific with Heimdall policies
- Uses `X-Rate-Limit-Group` header for use-case-specific limits
- Configuration via `service-descriptor.yml` + Shipyard Heimdall policies

### How It Applies to Tool Design
The wrapper should:
- Implement exponential backoff on429 responses
- Track `X-RateLimit-Remaining` header and pre-emptively throttle
- Document per-feature-view rate limits in tool spec
- For batch operations, prefer batch API endpoints to minimize request count

---

