## 5. IndustryFinOps Framework Patterns

### 5.1 FinOps Foundation Framework (as applied at Atlassian)

**What it is**: The three-pillar FinOps framework (from [Atlassian's FinOps Framework](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/CFINOPS/pages/3543186848)):

1. **Inform** — Visibility, allocation, benchmarking- Forecasting, Budgeting, Unit Economics, Onboarding
2. **Optimise** — Rate & usage optimization
   - Efficient Engineering, Integrated Platforms, Rate Optimization, Workload Optimization
3. **Manage** — Practice operations
   - Initiative Management, Education & Enablement, Vendor Management

### 5.2 FinOps Maturity Model for ML

**What it is**: Crawl-Walk-Run maturity levels (from [Cloud FinOps Maturity Model](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/~712020ab0daf1a29024b17ac3047b5ac2e5914/pages/3826194673)):

For **Load Testing/Experimentation**:
- **Crawl**: Each team owns experimentation workflows, gets approval from their respective verticals
- **Walk**: Approvals & forecasts tracked centrally byFinOps team
- **Run**: Budgeting for experimentation done per-team with allocated max budget; teams track expenses against budget

For **Anomaly Management**:
- **Crawl**: Manual investigation, routed to suspected owner
- **Walk**: Automated detection/reporting with tooling
- **Run**: Mature tooling embedded across org; analysis results in full RCCA/postmortem/future prevention

### 5.3 Unit Economics for ML

**What it is**: Breaking down costs into measurable units aligned with business value (from [FinOps: Unit Economics](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/CFINOPS/pages/3675985335)):

Key metrics for ML:
- **Cost per inference request** (online serving)
- **Cost per 1M tokens** (LLM inference)
- **Cost per training run** (GPU-hours × rate)
- **Cost per batch job** (data volume × processing rate)
- **Cost per MAU** (ML feature cost divided by monthly active users)

