## 3. Cost Anomaly Detection

### 3.1 Multi-Signal Detection Algorithm

**What it is**: Atlassian's production anomaly detection system using multiple statistical checks with confidence weighting (from [DailyFinOps Cost Anomaly Bot](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/~7120209bee8509eb8f4b2a9c982782b3e98a54/pages/5656283815)):

**How it works** —4-check ensemble with confidence weighting:

| Check | Method | Confidence Weight |
|-------|--------|------------------|
| 1. Prophet Check (ML) | 90days historical, weekly seasonality,95% CI; anomaly = actual > predicted upper | 0.4 |
| 2. Rolling % + threshold | Tier-based thresholds (micro/small/medium/large/very large) | 0.3 |
| 3. Statistical (Normal Distribution) | Current daily cost exceeds upper limit of distribution | 0.2 |
| 4. Ranking | Top 7 within last 90 days | 0.1 |

**Tier-based thresholds** for Rolling Check:
```python
base_thresholds = {
    'micro': {'pct': 80, 'abs': 30},       # ~$200/week
    'small': {'pct': 60, 'abs': 70},       # ~$500/week
    'medium': {'pct': 40, 'abs': 215},     # ~$1500/week
    'large': {'pct': 35, 'abs': 430},      # ~$3000/week
    'very large': {'pct': 25, 'abs': 715}  # ~$5000/week
}
```

**Alert Classification**:
- **Critical**: weight = 1.0 (all4 checks fire)
- **High**: weight > 0.5
- **Medium**: weight ≤ 0.5and 2 checks fire
- **Low**: remaining

### 3.2 Severity Matrix for Cost Incidents

**What it is**: Thresholds for escalating cost anomalies to incidents (from [Driving Efficiency: Cost Incident Program](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/CFINOPS/blog/2024/07/05/3903241042) and [Guideline - Incident Severity -FinOps](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/ish/pages/5193211217)):

| Cost Anomaly | Time to Resolve | Sev 3 | Sev 2 |
|--------------|-----------------|--------|--------|
| Cost Spike | Any | >$8K/week | >$40K/week |
| Cost Spike | >7 days TTR | >$3K/week | — |
| Attribution degradation | Any | >$8K/week unattributed | >$40K/week or >10% of platform spend |

**Workflow**:
1. Weekly automated detection → SAVE ticket created
2. 1-week SLA for service owner response
3. If unresolved after SLA → escalation to HOT incident
4. Weekly panel reviews sustained anomalies

### 3.3 Service-Level Anomaly Dimensions

**What it is**: Multi-dimensional anomaly detection across different scopes (from [Current Cost Anomaly Detection Mechanism](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/CFINOPS/pages/5330060104)):

| Dimension | Focus | Thresholds |
|-----------|-------|------------|
| AWS Region | Weekly cost by service_group + aws_product | Prophet95% CI, 20% change or $500absolute, 4-week lookback |
| AWS Product | Specific products (CloudWatch, KMS, Kinesis) | Prophet 95% CI, 40% change or $500 absolute, 4-week lookback |
| Service Group | All groups by service_name + aws_product | Prophet 95% CI, 40% change or $500 absolute, 10-week lookback |
| Service Name ($500-$10K) | By aws_region + aws_product | Prophet 95% CI, 60% change or $3000 absolute, 10-week lookback |
| Service Name (>$10K) | By aws_region + aws_product | Prophet 95% CI, $3000 absolute, 10-week lookback |

### 3.4 ML-Specific Anomaly Detection Needs

**What it is**: Additional detection patterns needed for ML workloads.

**From [TrustFinOps Process Uplift](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/~dkolbly/pages/5199477755)**:
```
An anomaly is signaled if:
- Daily cost driver variation exceeds 10% OR trailing 7-day variation exceeds 3%
- Daily usage variation exceeds 2%
- Fully loaded daily cost variation exceeds 2% AND more than $500
- Absolute variation more than $1000
- Absolute variation percentage more than 30% AND absolute variation more than $100
```

**From [AWS Cost Anomaly Detection Bot](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/~63ad4ea72c70aae1e6fa1f8d/pages/7009763352)**:
Configurable per cost-center thresholds:
| Cost Centre | DoD $ Threshold | MoM $ Threshold | % Threshold |
|-------------|-----------------|-----------------|-------------|
| Global default | $50,000 | $200,000 | 25% |
| Engineering-AI COGS | $100,000 | $400,000 | 20% |

**How it applies**:
For the AI employee's `cost_attribution_query` tool:
1. Per-experiment anomaly detection should use the tiered threshold model (scale thresholds based on experiment's historical spend level)
2. Detect both cost spikes AND utilization drops (idle GPU burning budget)
3. Alert latency: near-real-time for GPU costs (not the standard 3-day AWS billing delay) — use ML Fabric/TeamServe metrics instead
4. Human-in-the-loop: Autonomous alert for<$1K anomalies; require human confirmation for budget override >$5K

