# Deployment Manifest Schemas & Change-Management Patterns: Focused Domain Research

## Research Context
This research covers deployment manifest / change-management schemas (intent, non-goals, invariants, risk class, rollback, post-deploy monitoring) from SRE + MLOps practice, to inform the `deployment_manifest_generator` tool and `deployment-manifest-authoring` skill for an AI SRE employee.

---

## Finding 1: Atlassian Deploy Plan Template — Full Deployment Manifest Schema

### What it is
A production-grade, 12-section deployment manifest template actively used at Atlassian for all planned service/pod releases. This represents a real-world, battle-tested deployment manifest schema.

### How it works
The template has these structured sections:

1. **Release Overview** — Service/pod, version/tag, environment(s), release owner, change window
2. **Safety & Readiness Check** — Scope confirmation (all changes listed, no unresolved critical bugs, migrations approved, dependencies compatible), risk level (Low/Medium/High), known risks & mitigations, approvals
3. **Delta of PRs** — Previous vs. current release tag/commit, per-PR metadata (title, author, area/component, risk L/M/H, notes on migrations/flags, test status), exclusions with reasons
4. **Testing Status** — Unit tests, integration tests, E2E/UI tests, performance/load tests, manual/exploratory testing (scenarios, environments, sign-offs), outstanding gaps with risk mitigation
5. **Confidence Assessment** — Overall level (Very High/High/Medium/Low) with justification evidence
6. **Feature Flagging** — Per-change flag tracking (flag name, default state, rollout plan, rollback via flag Y/N), flag management owner
7. **Deployment Window & Steps** — Target time with timezone, expected duration, change window approval, deployment method (Rolling/Blue-green/Canary/Manual/Other), step-by-step runbook
8. **On-call / Monitoring Owner** — Primary and backup monitors (role, contact), on-call schedule link
9. **Monitors & Alerts During and After Release** — Key dashboards (health, errors, latency, resource utilization), critical alerts (metric, threshold, channel, action if breached), specific checks at three horizons: immediate (0-10mins), short-term (10-60 mins), extended (first 24 hours)
10. **Rollback Plan & Script** — Objective rollback conditions (error rate, latency thresholds), who can decide rollback (names/roles), technical rollback steps, data considerations (irreversible changes), validation after rollback, rollback testing confirmation
11. **Component Release Plan** — Per-component version/tag, release type (canary/full), dependency notes, owner, status, sequencing constraints, cross-pod coordination
12. **Communication Plan** — Pre-deploy comms, during-deploy updates (channel, cadence, owner), post-deploy summary

### How it applies
This is the primary schema reference for the `deployment_manifest_generator` tool. The tool should generate a structured JSON/YAML document that maps to these 12 sections. For the AI agent:
- **Autonomous execution (🟢)**: Sections 1, 3, 4, 5, 6, 8, 9 can be auto-populated from CI/CD metadata, Jira issues, and monitoring config
- **Human confirmation required (🟡)**: Sections 2 (risk assessment), 7 (timing), 10 (rollback), 12 (comms)
- **Human decision (🔴)**: Go/no-go decision after manifest review

### Source
[Deploy Plan - [Service / Pod] – [Release Version] – [Date]](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/DMP/pages/6344520323/Deploy+Plan+-+Service+Pod+Release+Version+Date) — Atlassian internal template, January 2026

