## 10. Autonomy Classification for Communication Actions

### What It Is
Mapping each communication action to the3-tier autonomy model (🟢/🟡/🔴) to determine what the AI employee can send autonomously vs. what requires human confirmation.

### How It Works

| Communication Action | Autonomy Tier | Rationale |
|---------------------|---------------|-----------|
| Post weekly digest to internal Slack channel | 🟢 Autonomous | Read-only summary, no external audience, easily correctable |
| Draft experiment conclusion packet | 🟡 Supervised | Contains recommendation that may influence decisions; human reviews before distribution |
| Send incident "Investigating" alert to internal channel | 🟢 Autonomous | Time-critical, factual, internal only |
| Post incident update to external Statuspage | 🔴 Human-required | External-facing, brand/trust implications, legal sensitivity |
| Draft PIR and circulate for review | 🟡 Supervised | Contains root cause analysis that may implicate teams; review before sending |
| Generate quarterly executive report | 🟡 Supervised | Strategic content that may inform leadership decisions |
| Escalate to leadership during incident | 🟡 Supervised | Should verify severity assessment before waking up executives |
| Send daily standup reminder/aggregation | 🟢 Autonomous | Routine, internal, no decision content |
| Notify model owner of drift alert | 🟢 Autonomous | Factual alert routing, time-sensitive |
| Announce model promotion to production | 🟡 Supervised | May need to verify deployment success before announcing |

### How It Applies
The `autonomy_tier_router` tool classifies each communication action. The `escalation_router` uses this classification to determine whether to send immediately (🟢), queue for human confirmation (🟡), or block and alert a human (🔴). The `stakeholder-communication-cadence` skill documents these classifications for the AI employee to follow.

