# Cumulative-Learning / Context-Layer /'Institutional Memory' Enrichment Patterns

## Research Summary

This research covers the patterns, fields, triggers, and deduplication strategies needed to design an `orbit_context_writer` tool and `orbit-enrichment` skill for an AI employee that "never solves the same problem twice." Findings are drawn from Atlassian's internal ORBIT platform, Jira AI's Distilled Knowledge system, Rovo Memory architecture, industry context graph research (Gartner, Glean, OpenAI), and the AI SRE/AutoSkill self-evolving patterns.

---

## 1. Multi-Layered Context Architecture (What to Store)

### Pattern: OpenAI's6-Layer Context Model
**What it is**: OpenAI's internal data agent uses six layers of progressively richer context to achieve near-perfect accuracy.

**How it works** (layers and accuracy lift):
| Level | Name | Contents | Cumulative Accuracy |
|-------|------|----------|-------------------|
| 1 | Technical metadata | Schemas, tables, columns, types, statistics | 10–20% |
| 2 | Relationships | PKs/FKs, join paths, cardinality | 30–40% |
| 3 | Catalog & business definitions | Glossaries, ownership, golden queries | 50–65% |
| 4 | Semantic layer | Metric definitions, business rules, ontologies | 75–85% |
| 5 | Tribal knowledge & memory | Intent, preferences, query reuse, feedback,RL | 94–99% |
| 6 | Runtime context | Live queries against data platform at inference time | — |

**How it applies to orbit_context_writer**: The tool should write enrichment records tagged by layer (L1–L5). Layer 5 (tribal knowledge + memory) is the cumulative-learning layer — what gets captured per task execution. Layer 6 is read-only runtime. The tool should primarily write L4–L5 data.

**Source**: [Context Layer Industry Trend](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/~7120207157313704204e298486e5b4e1e708c6/pages/6634473336/Context+Layer+Industry+Trend) (Promethium research + OpenAI data agent)

---

### Pattern: ORBIT's Three-Phase Context Model
**What it is**: ORBIT (Optimised Relevance, Bounded and Individually Tailored) is Atlassian's personalization + context layer that packages per-user or per-agent relevance projections from the Teamwork Graph.

**How it works**:
- **Context Training Layer**: Ingests signals from TwG +3P sources → ML ranking model → produces inferred entities (collaboration strength, authority signals, relevance ranks, focus-area fingerprints)
- **Context API Layer**: Stored in ML Feature Store, served via low-latency API as structured "context packs" — the right who/what/where/how for a given user/use-case/moment
- **Context Pack Properties**: Purpose-limited, budgeted, explainable, permission-safe

**Key design principles for an AI employee**:
- Context packs carry **reason codes** (why this is here)
- Users/operators can **pin, hide, or mark items "not relevant"** (feedback loop)
- Merges offline-calculated signals with TwG's live graph (online freshness)

**How it applies**: The `orbit_context_writer` should write enrichment that feeds back into ORBIT's training layer as a signal source. Each write should include a reason code explaining why this knowledge was captured.

**Source**: [ORBIT Primer](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/AICT/pages/6698145035/ORBIT+Primer), [AI Employee Strategy](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/~71202027e1af5d30e94112880666ee62f9277a/pages/6742955831/AI+Employee+Strategy)

---

## 2. What Fields to Capture Per Task (Schema Design)

### Pattern: Jira AI Distilled Knowledge Schema
**What it is**: Jira AI's production system for capturing reusable knowledge at the end of every agent session.

**Schema** (`ati:cloud:convo-ai:distilled-knowledge v1`):
| Field | Type | Description |
|-------|------|-------------|
| `projectAri` | string | Jira project ARI — stable identifier for scope |
| `agentId` | string | Agent identity |
| `contextRetrieval` | map | Links determined useful for this agent in this space |
| `content` | map | Specific pieces of content referenced and useful for the agent |
| `misc` | map | Preferences, decisions, pitfalls, patterns, corrections accumulated over sessions |
| `updatedAt` | long | Timestamp of last distillation |
| `modelVersion` | string | LLM version that produced this distillation |

**Index**: Hash on `projectAri` + `agentId`

**How it applies**: This is a direct template for the `orbit_context_writer`'s output schema. For a broader AI employee, extend with:
- `taskType` (incident, deployment, experiment, etc.)
- `outcomeStatus` (success/failure/partial)
- `confidenceScore` (calibrated uncertainty)
- `supersedes` (ARI of prior knowledge this replaces)

**Source**: [Distilled knowledge - backend architecture](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/~712020074b3a937c8447419f2bd08fcc30faf3/pages/7085077288/Distilled+knowledge+-+backend+architecture)

---

### Pattern: Decision Trace Fields (Context Graph)
**What it is**: The schema Atlassian is standardizing for capturing decision traces in the Teamwork Graph.

**Fields per decision**:
| Field | Description |
|-------|-------------|
| **Context** | Services, teams, initiative, severity, customer, time window, relevant metrics |
| **Choice made** | What option was selected and what alternatives were available |
| **Authority** | Who decided (role/team as well as individual) |
| **Rationale/label** | Short explanation or categorical reason (risk, SLA, VIP, regulatory, etc.) |
| **Outcome** | Whether this decision led to good/bad downstream results (for learning) |

**How it applies**: For the AI employee's `orbit_context_writer`, each task completion should emit a decision trace with these fields. Agents use these as **guardrails + priors** when proposing actions, deciding when to escalate, and checking whether the current situation falls outside prior decision envelopes.

**Source**: [Capturing Decision Traces as a Context Graph in Aura IQ](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/DEx/pages/6319296711/Capturing+Decision+Traces+as+a+Context+Graph+in+Aura+IQ)

---

### Pattern: Self-Evolving SRE Memory Fields
**What it is**: The AI SRE "One-Click" MVP captures per-event knowledge to build deep domain specialization.

**Per-event capture types**:
| Event Type | What is Captured |
|------------|-----------------|
| Every **incident** | Causal graph, permanent remediation PR proposed, spec constraint updated |
| Every **deployment** | Canary behavior observed, risk model updated for change types |
| Every **alert** | Tracked whether it led to real incident vs. false positive; signal-to-noise model tuned |
| Every **policy execution** | Reasoning trace logged; human review feedback captured |

**Key principle**: "Agents are generic at birth, specialists by assignment." After 30 days of accumulation, two instances assigned to different services become entirely different entities.

**How it applies**: The `orbit_context_writer` should have typed entry schemas per workflow domain (incident, deployment, alert, experiment). Each type captures domain-specific fields plus a common envelope.

**Source**: [Agentic Operations in the New SDLC](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/~71202027e1af5d30e94112880666ee62f9277a/pages/6885869718/Agentic+Operations+in+the+New+SDLC)

---

### Proposed Unified Enrichment Record Schema

Based on the patterns above, here is a synthesized schema for the `orbit_context_writer`:

```json
{
  "recordId": "uuid",
  "agentId": "string — AI employee identity",
  "scope": {
    "projectAri": "string — project/space this knowledge is scoped to",
    "serviceAri": "string? — optional service-level scope",
    "teamAri": "string? — optional team-level scope"
  },
  "taskContext": {
    "taskType": "enum: incident | deployment | experiment | alert | review | planning",
    "taskId": "string — Jira issue key, incident ID, deployment manifest ID",
    "triggeredAt": "ISO timestamp",
    "completedAt": "ISO timestamp",
    "outcomeStatus": "enum: success | failure | partial | escalated"
  },
  "knowledge": {
    "contextRetrieval": ["list of useful links/ARIs discovered"],
    "patterns": ["reusable patterns discovered — how to solve this class of problem"],
    "pitfalls": ["anti-patterns, dead ends, things that failed"],
    "decisions": [{
      "choice": "what was decided",
      "alternatives": ["what else was considered"],
      "rationale": "why this choice",
      "authority": "who/what approved",
      "outcome": "result observed"
    }],
    "corrections": ["human corrections to agent behavior"],
    "preferences": ["stable instructions from humans about how to operate"]
  },
  "provenance": {
    "reasonCode": "string — why this was captured",
    "confidenceScore": 0.0-1.0,
    "modelVersion": "string — LLM that produced distillation",
    "supersedes": "recordId? — prior record this replaces",
    "sourceTraceId": "string — execution trace ID for auditability"
  },
  "lifecycle": {
    "createdAt": "ISO timestamp",
    "updatedAt": "ISO timestamp",
    "accessCount": 0,
    "lastAccessedAt": "ISO timestamp?",
    "ttlDays": "integer? — null means evergreen",
    "isEvergreen": false
  }
}
```

