# Day-One Readiness / Onboarding Bootstrap Checklists & L1/L2→L3 Promotion-Gate Criteria for Autonomous-Agent Roles

## Research Summary

This research synthesizes findings from 15+ internal Atlassian sources and industry frameworks to inform the `day-one-readiness-bootstrap` skill design. The findings cover: (a) what an autonomous agent needs before it can start work (bootstrap checklist), (b) how agents progress through maturity levels (promotion gates), and (c) the evidence-based criteria that gate each transition.

---

## 1. Day-One Readiness Bootstrap Checklist for Autonomous Agents

### 1.1 The Atlassian Pattern: "One-Click AI SRE" Bootstrap Protocol

**What it is:** A proven bootstrap protocol where a single URL triggers complete agent setup in seconds.

**How it works** (from [SRE Agent Primer](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/LAB/pages/6973837886/SRE+Agent+Primer)):
1. Agent fetches a centrally-hosted prototype file (role definition)
2. Auto-configures local tools (atlas CLI, uv, npm, Databricks, Splunk)
3. Hooks into local telemetry for session tracking
4. Reads the repo to understand service dependencies and traffic patterns
5. Over-the-air upgrades on each new session (diffs prototype, pulls delta)

**Key principle:** "After hire, the agent drives. The human checkpoints exist to maintain oversight at trust boundaries — direction-setting and final approval — while the agent handles everything in between, continuously."

**How it applies to skill design:** The `day-one-readiness-bootstrap` skill should encode this exact protocol as a step-by-step workflow, with validation gates at each step.

**Source:** [SRE Agent Primer](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/LAB/pages/6973837886/SRE+Agent+Primer)

---

### 1.2 Agent Lifecycle Bootstrap Phases

**What it is:** The canonical agent lifecycle from generic to deep specialist.

**How it works** (from [Agentic Operations in the New SDLC](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/~71202027e1af5d30e94112880666ee62f9277a/pages/6885869718/Agentic+Operations+in+the+New+SDLC)):

```
Initiation → [Generic Agent] — core skills, no domain knowledge↓
Assignment to service / repo / team
    ↓
[Bootstrap Training] — ingests Spec, incident history, runbooks, ORBIT context pack
    ↓
[Active Ops] — enforces policies, files Causal Graphs, proposes Permanent Remediation PRs
    ↓
[Self-Learning] — updates domain model, tunes signal thresholds, encodes lessons to Spec
    ↓
[Deep Specialist] — world's foremost expert on this service after N months
```

**Bootstrap Training ingests:**
- ORBIT context pack (ML-ranked view of what's active, at-risk, and owned)
- Service Spec (Intent Map) — canonical description of service behavior
- Incident history — past HOTs, postmortems, Causal Graphs
- Runbooks and policy expressions — human-authored playbooks → machine-executable policies
- Observability config — alert definitions, SLO thresholds, dashboard layouts

**Critical insight:** "If a service has good existing documentation, the agent is productive on Day 1. If the service has poor documentation, the agent's first job is to *generate that documentation*."

**Source:** [Agentic Operations in the New SDLC](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/~71202027e1af5d30e94112880666ee62f9277a/pages/6885869718/Agentic+Operations+in+the+New+SDLC)

---

### 1.3 ORBIT Context Pack — Cold-Start Elimination

**What it is:** A personalized, ML-scored context package that eliminates agent ramp time.

**How it works** (from [Symphony](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/~71202027e1af5d30e94112880666ee62f9277a/pages/6933824809/Atlassian+Symphony+AI+Employee+Orchestration+Platform)):
- ORBIT loads a context pack scoped to the work item
- For SRE: service architecture + incident history + SLOs
- For SWE: repo context + PR history + test coverage
- For MLE: model lineage + experiment history + data quality signals

**Context Pack v1 Required Properties** (from [ORBIT RFC](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/~71202049b56fb3ef7b49abbbe4f53dbbf12160/pages/6272801311/ORBIT+-+Personalization+Context+Packaging+Layer+for+Rovo)):
| Field Group | Contents |
|---|---|
| Identity | tenantId, userId, packId, schemaVersion |
| Purpose & TTL | purpose, createdAt, ttl/expiry |
| Budget | maxEntities, maxArtifacts, maxEdges, tokenBudget |
| Entities | TwG entity references with layer, score, and reasons |
| Artifacts | typed references (tickets/docs/repos/PRs/incidents) |
| Graph slice | minimal edges among included items |
| Policy & audit | permission checks, provenance, policy decisions |

**How it applies:** The bootstrap skill should validate ORBIT context pack availability and completeness as a pre-flight check. Missing ORBIT = fallback to manual context assembly.

**Source:** [ORBIT - Personalization & Context Packaging Layer](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/~71202049b56fb3ef7b49abbbe4f53dbbf12160/pages/6272801311/ORBIT+-+Personalization+Context+Packaging+Layer+for+Rovo)

---

### 1.4 Consolidated Day-One Readiness Checklist (Synthesized Pattern)

Based on all sources, here is the canonical bootstrap checklist structure:

#### Phase 0: Pre-Flight Validation (Silent, before agent activates)
- [ ] **Provisioning check** — Agent app/service is provisioned and active
- [ ] **Credit/budget check** — Sufficient compute/token budget allocated
- [ ] **Identity established** — Agent has its own identity with least-privilege scope
- [ ] **Sandbox available** — Remote execution environment configured
- [ ] **ORBIT context pack** — Cold-start context loaded (or fallback initiated)

#### Phase 1: Role Definition & Policy Loading
- [ ] **Role definition fetched** — What the agent knows, is responsible for, how it should behave
- [ ] **Policy scope loaded** — 🟢/🟡/🔴 autonomy tiers defined for this role
- [ ] **Trust boundaries explicit** — Which actions are autonomous vs. confirm vs. escalate
- [ ] **Audit trail configured** — All actions logged with reasoning traces
- [ ] **Escalation paths defined** — Default-to-page if no policy matches

#### Phase 2: Toolchain & Integration Setup
- [ ] **Tools auto-configured** — CLI tools, SDKs, API access verified
- [ ] **Telemetry hooked** — Session tracking, usage reporting active
- [ ] **Communication channels** — Slack/Teams channels identified and joined
- [ ] **Control plane access** — Jira/JSM/ML platform connections verified
- [ ] **Knowledge sources indexed** — Runbooks, specs, incident history accessible

#### Phase 3: Domain Context Acquisition
- [ ] **Service/repo read** — Dependencies, traffic patterns understood
- [ ] **Incident history ingested** — Past HOTs, PIRs, causal graphs
- [ ] **SLO/SLA awareness** — Thresholds, error budgets loaded
- [ ] **Team roster mapped** — Stakeholders, escalation contacts identified
- [ ] **Documentation audit** — Gaps identified; if critical gaps, generate docs first

#### Phase 4: Readiness Verification
- [ ] **Dry-run execution** — Shadow mode test on recent historical work item
- [ ] **Output validation** — SME confirms agent output quality
- [ ] **Confidence calibration** — Baseline confidence thresholds set
- [ ] **Upgrade mechanism** — OTA update path verified
- [ ] **Bootstrap complete marker** — Persistent state written

**Source:** Synthesized from [SRE Agent Primer](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/LAB/pages/6973837886/SRE+Agent+Primer), [AI SRE Bootstrap Skill Spec](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/AIDO/pages/6821328940/AI+SRE+Birth+certificate+Bootstrap+Skill+Spec), [Agentic Operations](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/~71202027e1af5d30e94112880666ee62f9277a/pages/6885869718/Agentic+Operations+in+the+New+SDLC)

---

## 2. Maturity-Promotion Gates: L1/L2 → L3 Criteria

### 2.1 The SRE Agent Maturity Model (Primary Internal Framework)

**What it is:** The authoritative L0-L4 maturity model for SRE agents at Atlassian.

**How it works** (from [SRE Agent Maturity Model](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/BSRE/pages/7058433005/SRE+Agent+Maturity+Model)):

| Level | Capability | Persona | Metrics | Necessary Capabilities for Next Level |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| **L0** | Humans use LLM Skills locally | Chat Assistant | "Vibes" — SME agrees with output | Remote Agent Sandbox, Agent Harness |
| **L1** | Triggered manually, runs in sandbox, recommends actions | Researcher/Helper | Output acceptance rate; turns until accepted | Consistent Context Engineering, Audit Traces, Reinforcement Signal visibility |
| **L2** | Triggered automatically, auto-remediates some tasks, HITL | Untrusted Worker | HITL approval rate; Output acceptance rate | Automated Eval harness, Model Drift detection |
| **L3** | Takes any allowed action, no HITL | Teammate | Eval success rate | Self-improvement from reinforcement signals |
| **L4** | Coordinates SDLC and Ops | Digital Employee | Agentic capability over time | — |

**Critical philosophy:** "Moving down the capability model without first addressing governance is a surefire recipe for unmitigated disaster. These risks are financial, legal, and reputational."

**Key insight on L2→L3 transition:** "Human observation and just-in-time intervention slowly gets replaced by more abstract reinforcement signals. The early maturity levels rely on reinforcement for individual suggestions and actions, but later maturity levels rely on reinforcement of work output."

**Source:** [SRE Agent Maturity Model](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/BSRE/pages/7058433005/SRE+Agent+Maturity+Model)

---

### 2.2 RFC-002 — AI SDLC Governance Framework (Org-Wide Standard)

**What it is:** The cross-Atlassian governance framework with evidence-based progression gates.

**How it works** (from [RFC-002](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/AISDLC/pages/6849219616/RFC-002+-+AI+SDLC+Governance+Framework)):

| Level | Name | AI Role | Human Decision Authority |
|---|---|---|---|
| L0 | No AI | Absent | Humans do all work |
| L1 | Assistive | Suggests/drafts | Humans review all outputs |
| L2 | Structured assistance | Performs bounded tasks | Humans retain explicit approval on every action |
| **L3** | **Conditional autonomy** | **Executes in low-risk cases** | **Humans oversee exceptions and sampled outcomes** |
| L4 | High autonomy | Handles most tasks | Humans govern policy, thresholds, incident response |
| L5 | Near-full autonomy | Primary decision-maker | Humans provide governance, audits, emergency intervention |

**5Explicit Progression Gates** (evidence-based, not self-declared):
1. **Concordance** — AI outputs agree with human judgment at a threshold rate
2. **Failure rate parity** — Autonomous actions fail no more often than human-performed ones
3. **Calibration** — Agent confidence scores correlate with actual outcome accuracy
4. **Coverage** — Eval infrastructure covers the workflows being delegated
5. **Feedback loops** — Errors are captured, analyzed, and drive model/prompt improvements

**Readiness caps Maturity:** R1 readiness caps maturity at L2. You cannot be L3 without R2 readiness.

| Readiness | Description |
|---|---|
| R0 | Not Ready |
| R1 | Foundational — basic infra |
| R2 | Enabled — eval, monitoring, documentation in place |
| R3 | Optimized — continuous improvement |

**Design Principles:**
- "Risk-bounded autonomy: AI decision-making authority must be capped by the risk profile of the work being performed"
- "Evidence-based progression: Advancement requires observable evidence"
- "No single aggregate score: maturity profiles across domains rather than a blended number"

**Source:** [RFC-002 - AI SDLC Governance Framework](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/AISDLC/pages/6849219616/RFC-002+-+AI+SDLC+Governance+Framework)

---

### 2.3 Code Review Maturity Model Progression Logic (Decision Tree)

**What it is:** A concrete decision tree for level progression.

**How it works** (from [Maturity Model for AI in Code Review](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/~cknott/pages/6677006234/A+Maturity+Model+for+AI+in+Code+Review+From+Human+Oversight+to+Machine+Governance)):

```
Q1: Can we trace what agents did and why?→ NO: Stay at L0/L1 — build traceability first→ YES → Q2: Do we have risk classification, change manifests, and an AI reviewer?
    → NO: Stay at L1 — build risk classification
    → YES → Q3: Do low-risk autonomous approvals have a low failure rate?
      → NO: Stay at L2 — improve evidence model
      → YES → Q4: Specialized reviewers + executable policy + deployment integration?
        → NO: Stay at L3 — build specialized reviewers
        → YES → Q5: Autonomous incident rates≤ human-approved?
          → NO: Stay at L3 — reduce incident rate
          → YES → Q5b: Specialized reviewers demonstrate domain catches?
            → YES → Q6: Runtime containment catches regressions before harm?
              → YES: Level 5
              → NO: Stay at L4
```

**L2→L3 gate specifically requires:**
- Low failure rate for autonomously approved changes
- Reliable audit trail
- AI reviewer consistently catches issues that human review would catch

**Source:** [A Maturity Model for AI in Code Review](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/~cknott/pages/6677006234/A+Maturity+Model+for+AI+in+Code+Review+From+Human+Oversight+to+Machine+Governance)

---

### 2.4 MLE Agent Capability Evaluation — Concrete L1/L2/L3 Gap Analysis

**What it is:** A real-world assessment mapping tasks to current agency levels and identifying gaps to L3.

**How it works** (from [MLE Agent Capability Evaluation](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/~7120202437cfa6229b4f95a58ffcf2c483eb04/pages/6983354773/MLE+Agent+Capability+Evaluation+2026-05-06)):

Each task is assessed on:
- **Coverage** (❌/🟡/✅) — Does the agent have access to this capability?
- **Completion** (%) — How far can the agent currently go?
- **Quality** (Low/Medium/High) — How good is the output?
- **Agency** (L1/L2/L3) — Current autonomy level
- **Gaps to L3** — What's missing

**Example assessments:**
| Task | Current Agency | Gaps to L3 |
|---|---|---|
| Monitor live model in SignalFx | L1 | No "what to watch" checklist |
| Promote model EXPERIMENTAL → RELEASED | L1 | No thresholds, no approval flow |
| Roll back a bad model version | L1 | No rollback runbook |
| Update project.md after promotion | L2 | Service wiring handoff undocumented |

**Roadmap to L3:**
- Quick wins (1–2 sprints): Write missing runbooks, define eval rubrics, document handoffs
- Medium-term (3–6 sprints): Build orchestrator, implement SLO detection, multi-region playbooks

**How it applies:** The bootstrap skill should include a "capability gap audit" step that maps current state to L3 requirements and identifies blocking gaps.

**Source:** [MLE Agent Capability Evaluation](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/~7120202437cfa6229b4f95a58ffcf2c483eb04/pages/6983354773/MLE+Agent+Capability+Evaluation+2026-05-06)

---

### 2.5 Symphony3-Tier Autonomy Framework (Policy-Level Gates)

**What it is:** The operational autonomy model that governs all AI employees.

**How it works** (from [Symphony](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/~71202027e1af5d30e94112880666ee62f9277a/pages/6933824809/Atlassian+Symphony+AI+Employee+Orchestration+Platform)):

| Tier | Signal | Meaning | Gate Criteria |
|---|---|---|---|
| 1 — Autonomous | 🟢 | Act without approval | Known pattern, within policy scope, bounded blast radius |
| 2 — Confirm | 🟡 | Propose with reasoning, human approves | Novel patterns or higher-stakes decisions |
| 3 — Escalate | 🔴 | Page human with full context | Hard policy boundary hit |

**Promotion from🟡 to 🟢 for a specific action requires:**
- Pattern is known (seen N times with consistent positive outcomes)
- Within policy scope (version-controlled, auditable, human-authored)
- Bounded blast radius (reversible or contained)
- Human-authored policy explicitly permits the action

**Key constraint:** "Symphony's autonomy is bounded by what humans have explicitly permitted; it never exceeds it."

**Source:** [Atlassian Symphony](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/~71202027e1af5d30e94112880666ee62f9277a/pages/6933824809/Atlassian+Symphony+AI+Employee+Orchestration+Platform)

---

### 2.6 Google SRE On-Call Onboarding Pattern (Industry Standard)

**What it is:** The structured progression path for SRE onboarding, adaptable to agents.

**How it works** (from [Google SRE Book Summary](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/AIDO/pages/6798517849/Google+SRE+Book+Summary+AI+SRE+Agent+Design+Implications)):

```
Structured learning → Shadowing → Reverse shadowing → Solo on-call with escalation paths
```

**AI agent equivalent:**
- **Structured learning** = Bootstrap Training (ingest specs, runbooks, incident history)
- **Shadowing** = Shadow mode (classify/recommend but don't act; compare to human decisions)
- **Reverse shadowing** = Agent proposes, human approves (L2 /🟡 tier)
- **Solo on-call** = Autonomous execution for known patterns (L3 / 🟢 tier)

**Automation maturity stages (Ch7):**
| Stage | Description | AI SRE Position |
|---|---|---|
| 1 | No automation — manual | — |
| 2 | External scripts, run manually | — |
| 3 | System-specific automation by SRE | — |
| 4 | Automation with human decisions | Day1 target |
| 5 | Autonomous self-healing | North Star |

**Source:** [Google SRE Book Summary](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/AIDO/pages/6798517849/Google+SRE+Book+Summary+AI+SRE+Agent+Design+Implications)

---

### 2.7 Autonomous Agent Trust Principles (Governance Layer)

**What it is:** The six core principles that gate all autonomous agent actions.

**How it works** (from [Autonomous Agents: Legal and Trust Principles](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/~70121abadae569b3943e0bb23c17755833a90/pages/6671956189/Autonomous+Agents+Legal+and+Trust+Principles+V1)):

1. 🛡️ **Admin control** — Admins approve agent creation, access, capabilities; least-privilege default
2. 📝 **Auditability** — Log every action: who/what acted, what changed, where, when
3. 🧑‍💻 **Agent identity & accountability** — Label every agent with name, owner, admin, capabilities
4. 👀 **Human oversight for high-risk actions** — Destructive/irreversible actions require human approval
5. 🔄 **Reversibility** — Easyundo or versioned rollback where feasible
6. 🔧 **3P auth & delegation** — Show acting identity for external tool auth

**These are non-negotiable prerequisites for any level transition.** An agent cannot advance from L2→L3 without ALL six principles being demonstrably satisfied.

**Source:** [Autonomous Agents: Legal and Trust Principles](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/~70121abadae569b3943e0bb23c17755833a90/pages/6671956189/Autonomous+Agents+Legal+and+Trust+Principles+V1)

---

## 3. Synthesized L1/L2→L3 Promotion Gate Criteria

### 3.1 The Promotion Gate Framework (Consolidated)

Based on all sources, here is the synthesized promotion gate structure:

#### L0 → L1 Gate: "Can the agent run safely in isolation?"
| Criterion | Evidence Required |
|---|---|
| Remote sandbox exists | Agent runs in isolated environment, not on operator machine |
| Own identity | Agent has dedicated identity with least-privilege scoping |
| Agent harness | Structured invocation, no ad-hoc prompting |
| SME output validation | At least one trained SME agrees agent output is correct |

#### L1 → L2 Gate: "Can the agent be trusted to act with human approval?"
| Criterion | Evidence Required | Threshold |
|---|---|---|
| Consistent context engineering | ORBIT pack or equivalent provides stable, relevant context | Context pack available for >90% of tasks |
| Audit traces | Every action logged with reasoning chain | 100% trace coverage |
| Reinforcement signal visibility | Humans can see and correct agent decisions | Feedback UI active |
| Output acceptance rate | Outputs accepted without major revision | ≥80% acceptance |
| Turns to acceptance | Minimal back-and-forth to reach acceptable output | ≤2 turns average |

#### L2 → L3 Gate: "Can the agent act autonomously for known patterns?"⭐

| Criterion | Evidence Required | Threshold |
|---|---|---|
| **Automated eval harness** | Repeatable offline evals covering all autonomous workflows | ≥90% workflow coverage |
| **Model drift detection** | Active monitoring for output quality degradation | Alerts configured,<24h detection |
| **HITL approval rate** | High proportion of proposed actions approved without modification | ≥95% approval rate over 30-day window |
| **Failure rate parity** | Autonomous actions produce failures≤ human-performed equivalent | Agent failure rate ≤ human baseline |
| **Concordance** | Agent decisions match expert judgment |≥85% concordance on eval set |
| **Calibration** | Confidence scores correlate with actual outcomes | ECE < 0.10 |
| **Bounded blast radius** | All autonomous actions are reversible or contained | 100% of🟢-tier actions have rollback path |
| **Policy coverage** | Version-controlled policies exist for all autonomous actions | No policy gaps for >30days |
| **Governance compliance** | All6 trust principles satisfied | Audit confirms compliance |
| **Shadow mode success** | Extended period of correct shadow-mode recommendations | ≥2 weeks shadow mode with≥90% accuracy |

#### L3 → L4 Gate: "Can the agent coordinate with other agents?"
| Criterion | Evidence Required |
|---|---|
| Self-improvement from reinforcement signals | Agent improves over time without manual prompt updates |
| Multi-agent coordination | Successfully delegates to/receives from specialized agents |
| Eval success rate sustained | Maintains high eval scores over extended period (≥90days) |

---

### 3.2 The "Digital TPM" Comparison Pattern

**What it is:** A concrete before/after showing what separates an L1-L2 agent from an L3+ agent.

**How it works** (from [Digital TPM Vision](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/~71202027e1af5d30e94112880666ee62f9277a/pages/6900978790/Digital+TPM+AI+Employee+Vision)):

| Capability | L1-L2 (hire_a_tpm kit) | L3+ (Digital TPM) |
|---|---|---|
| SOPs + trust model | ✅ 3 encoded SOPs, 3-tier autonomy | ✅ Same — expands as trust is earned |
| Program context on Day 1 | ⚠️ Must be manually provided | ✅ ORBIT context pack auto-loaded |
| Self-learning | ❌ No persistent memory | ✅ Persistent memory deepens over time |
| Proactive operation | ⚠️ Reactive — user invocation | ✅ Scheduled cadence, surfaces blockers without being asked |
| Role specialization | ❌ Generic kit | ✅ Assigned to scope, specializes to risk patterns |

**North star metric:** "Time-to-Delegation — how quickly does the team trust the agent to run autonomously?"

**Source:** [Digital TPM Vision](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/~71202027e1af5d30e94112880666ee62f9277a/pages/6900978790/Digital+TPM+AI+Employee+Vision)

---

## 4. Industry Patterns: Progression Principles

### 4.1 Evidence-Based Progression (Not Self-Declared)

From [Core Eng AI Maturity Model](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/CoreEngineering/pages/7046656407/Core+Eng+AI+Maturity+Model+V1):

- "If you can't demonstrate it to a peer, you're not at that stage"
- "Stage transitions are investments, not switches"
- "Close gating gaps first — a team at Stage 4 on capability but Stage 1 on Safety is not advanced, it's exposed"
- "Not every team needs the highest level"

### 4.2 Guardrails as Hard Prerequisites

From [Agentic Operations in the New SDLC](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/~71202027e1af5d30e94112880666ee62f9277a/pages/6885869718/Agentic+Operations+in+the+New+SDLC):

| Failure Mode | Risk | Required Guardrail Before L3 |
|---|---|---|
| Misdiagnosis + wrong fix | HIGH | All fixes staged until reasoning trace reviewed |
| Policy gap — no matching pattern | HIGH | Default-to-page (silence is never default) |
| Remediation introduces new failure | MEDIUM | Full validate-stage eval before merge |
| Policy drift | MEDIUM | Versioned policies + governance rotation |
| Human loses ability to validate | MEDIUM | Deep-dive rotations maintain technical grounding |

### 4.3 The "AI-First Support Transformation" Pattern

From [AI-First Support Transformation Vision](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/~71202056fd0320303d4140aabd4a9d3138668a/pages/6657839223/AI-First+Support+Transformation+Vision):

"Human-in-the-loop until agents earn green zones; clear handoffs and guardrails."

Pattern: Shadow mode → confidence calibration → citations → green zones (autonomous).

